  ("Mental rotation", "Turning a representation in mind to compare how its parts line up, in the picture plane or in depth."),
  "Shepard and Metzler (1971) found that reaction time rose approximately linearly with the angle of rotation, so larger turns took longer. A larger angle needs a larger mental transformation before the objects can be compared, which suggests an active operation on a spatial representation.",
  "People judged whether pairs of block-built objects matched, and small angles were answered faster than large ones.",
